A core member of the You Can See Everything filmmaking team took part in an audience Q&A following the Elizabeth Holmes documentary's screening at TorunCamerimage in Poland, and the mantra appeared to: You can ask everything.

After Friday's screening of directors Nathan Fielder and Lance Oppenheim &#8216;s A24 movie that has become an Oscar-buzzed sensation, cinematographer David Bolen took the stage to answer questions from the moderator and then the audience. The conversation ran well over an hour, and the members of the crowd were encouraged throughout the event to continue asking questions about the experience in making the film about the disgraced Theranos founder. Toward the end, Bolen noted in regards to the amount of questions, &#8220;This is the craziest Q&A of all time.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories General News From Venice Winner 'Woman Unknown' and Westeros to 'Star City': Meet Some of the Baltic Craft Specialists Who've Worked on Int'l Productions Movies Robert Pattinson's Bondmaxxing: How 007 Still Has Pop Culture in a Vice Grip

Bolen responded to not one but two questions about the infamous moment in the film in which Holmes' partner Billy Evans is shown naked in the shower. &#8220;That scene is really interesting because, for us as filmmakers, it's like, this is truly a scene that depicts you can see everything,&#8221; Bolen said. &#8220;We're not trying to hide anything, and I think that's why it's interesting in the film is, it's these people who are trying to maybe depict, &#8216;We're totally open books here.' I'm curious what you people think because when shooting it, we're all like, &#8216;I don't really understand this.'&#8221;

In footage from his CBS Sunday Morning interview released that same day, Evans claimed that he did not consent to being fully nude and that he asked the filmmakers to pixelate the scene, but Bolen did not address such concerns. &#8220;I don't know the full context of how that all went down, but I think that's for the editor and Nathan,&#8221; Bolen told the crowd later about the shower scene. &#8220;I'm sure they've debated about every single scene moment that's in this film. And if it's in here, there's a reason that they put it in. So I don't think it's just for shock value. I'm sure there's reasons why Nathan thinks this is important to how we're depicting that.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Bolen did acknowledge that there was tension between Evans and the directors, both during filming and after the project had been completed. &#8220;It was sometimes a tense environment,&#8221; Bolen recalled. He shared that he would exchange pleasantries with Holmes and Evans, but that was generally the extent of their conversations: &#8220;I really tried to stay out of the situation as much as possible. Sometimes it would get tense, and you see in the office scene, it gets a little bit tense, and [I would] just try to be quiet and polite and get the cameras out.&#8221;

When asked how Evans felt about the film, Bolen replied, &#8220;[For] Billy, I was never the main person in contact with him. I believe Nathan and Lance, as it was coming out, it was tense between them. So I don't think they showed it to him [beforehand]. I think he saw it after the film had been released.&#8221;

You Can See Everything has become known for its extreme close-ups, and the cinematographer explained that this aspect of the film came about in the moment, with Oppenheim continually giving suggestions to Bolen and camera operator Jonathan Mitchell. &#8220;I remember we were filming the scene when [Fielder is] talking to Billy outside about how he met Elizabeth, and Lance was like, &#8216;Just go really close. Let's just try,'&#8221; said Bolen. &#8220;We were like, &#8216;Whoa, that's really interesting.' If we were just framing these human faces, it all becomes an experience of just trying to understand what's going on with the situation. Hopefully, it wasn't too claustrophobic.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Fielder was not initially intending to appear in the film but, according to Bolen, &#8220;What would happen is, it'd be kind of boring. We'd be filming them, [and] they'd be in the living room; they'd be sitting on a couch; they're playing with the baby. You're like, &#8216;OK, when are we going to get to the interesting stuff?' Slowly but surely, we would start handing the camera to Nathan.&#8221;

Bolen also recalled that the team wouldn't always know which moments to capture, with an example being the viral scene involving Evans taking a bowl of eggs away from Holmes so that he could be the one to whisk them. &#8220;Even the egg scene, I love that scene, but if you actually watch it, it's a little sloppy,&#8221; Bolen said. &#8220;I'm not perfect on everything, but luckily, with the three cameras, where I mess up, Jonathan has a shot. Where he messes up, I have a shot.&#8221;

The logic behind certain prequels is more organic than the logic behind others. The original "Star Wars" was retitled "Star Wars: Episode IV &#x2014; A New Hope" because it took place in the middle of something. That made the "Star Wars" prequels a valid idea, even if their execution was subpar.

But the logic behind creating a prequel to " The Hunger Games " series was simply that the franchise was too big to end. And maybe that's why three years ago, "The Hunger Games: The Ballad of Songbirds & Snakes" was such a stilted, cumbersome travesty of a movie. The film's storytelling problems went right back to the book (it was the worst of Suzanne Collins' novels), but the way the book translated to the big screen, it's as if someone had set forth a challenge: Create a blockbuster movie in which a young man who will grow up to be a famous villain &#x2014; in this case, Coriolanus Snow &#x2014; is even less convincing as a seedling of evil than Anakin Skywalker. That's what "The Ballad of Songbirds & Snakes" brought off. It scored at the box office but was a prequel without psychology, momentum, or dramatic sense. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Benjamín Vicuña, Star of Netflix Hit Series 'Envious,' Joins ‘My Last Stand' by Goya-Winner Matías Bize 'Woman Unknown' Breakout Mathilde Arcel on Her Venice Award-Winning Performance and Life After Her Lido Triumph: 'Everything Is Open'

All of which is to say: " The Hunger Games: Sunrise on the Reaping " had nowhere to go but up. Based on the second of Collins' two prequel novels, the film is meandering and more than a bit thin, but unlike "The Ballad of Songbirds & Snakes" it's been directed, by Francis Lawrence, in that lavish mode of surface intensity we might now call Hunger Games Classic. It tells the origin story of Haymitch Abernathy, the champion of the 50th Hunger Games, whom we met in the first "Hunger Games" movie as a drunken, gone-to-seed smart-mouth good guy played by Woody Harrelson. Though it was impossible to believe that blah Tom Blyth would grow up to be sinister President Snow, if you squint hard enough you can half-believe that Joseph Zada , who plays the 16-year-old Haymitch, might one day turn into Harrelson's dissolute former kid-champion celebrity. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

That said, for most of &#8220;Sunrise on the Reaping,&#8221; Zada, with his two-tone curly blond hair and blue-eyed stare of earnest valor, comes off like a taller version of Christopher Atkins in "The Blue Lagoon." As he enters the woodland arena and sets about trying to survive the film's teen-vs.-teen rendition of "The Most Dangerous Truman Show," he's the young warrior as pin-up.

One of the movie's better scenes is the reaping, which occurs on the same day (each year) as Haymitch's birthday. There are several violent surprises in it, and Glenn Close has a debauched good time playing Drusilla Sickle, the Capitol escort for the District 12 tributes, who presents as a regal cosmetic-surgery victim who just arrived from Oz on her way to the Met Gala. Haymitch is chosen to be one of District 12's four tributes to replace the reluctant tribute he tried, valiantly, to save. He will be our hero and tour guide in a movie that essentially replays the plot of the original "Hunger Games" movie.

Haymitch must leave behind his loving girlfriend, Lenore Dove Baird (Whitney Peak), but they're so devoted to each other that he's never tempted into a flirtation with Maysilee Donner ( Mckenna Grace ), one of the two female tributes from District 12. That's one of many ways the film clamps down on its drama.

It's all about the fascist media politics, you see. Kieran Culkin puts on an ugly mustache to play the young Caesar Flickerman (Culkin is fine, but not nearly as stylishly depraved as Stanley Tucci), and Haymitch meets Plutarch Heavensbee ( Jesse Plemons ), the up-and-coming cameraman for the Hunger Games (the character originally played by Philip Seymour Hoffman). Plutarch's job is to broadcast, but the crucial thing he does is to edit . What the viewers see at home, from the opening ceremonies onward, is the papered-over fake-news version, with awkward incidents left on the cutting-room floor. Plutarch, though, is also a decent chap who quietly encourages Haymitch to revolt. Haymitch has a close encounter with President Snow, the tyrant of Panem, now played by Ralph Fiennes, who leaves the late Donald Sutherland's grinning malice behind to play the character like George Washington as a corporate psycho. Haymitch mocked Snow at the opening ceremonies, so Snow has marked him for death. Have no fear: The two will make a deal. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

A big part of the folly of the "Hunger Games" prequels is that they're about the "evolution" of a dystopia&#x2026;that's already a dystopia! (How much of a thrill is it to see things slide from bad to a little bit worse?) The excitement, of course, is supposed to be there in the Second Quarter Quell, which opens with Haymitch and the other tributes being fed into a sunlit paradise that looks like the Shire attached to a Disney forest. Haymitch has a bomb he's going to set off, and the logistics this time involve an erupting volcano and a lot of ominous and mechanical creatures: glittery blue butterflies that can electrocute you, killer rabbits, red birds that attack like something out of &#8220;The Birds.&#8221;

The movie is, of course , two hours and 38 minutes long, and its distended final section, after Haymitch has won the Games (a reminder for novices: not a spoiler), is devoted to his "tragedy" and the way he devolves into the person Harrelson's Haymitch will be, in the movie that's set 24 years after this one. But a sunlit epilogue, featuring famous actors I won't name, let's you know: It's all good. As long as there are no more prequels to "The Hunger Games," I'd say that's a fair assessment.

Not so long ago, the old it's-funny-because-it's-true joke about soap operas was that people in soap operas never seemed to watch soap operas. But now that reality shows have all but taken over that space, the function of soaps has evaporated almost entirely, while "unscripted TV" has absorbed all of its clich&#xE9;s. When it first appeared in 2012, The Hunger Games seemed fresh and even satirical; now, though, it just feels tired, and this sixth lackluster offering raises only one serious question: Hasn't anyone forced to take part in a violent dystopic game show ever seen a violent dystopic game show before? blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Aha, you might say. This is a prequel , taking place a full 24 years before the original. True, the citizens of Panem have only had to put up with 49 editions of the Hunger Games thus far, but, leaving aside the ancient art of combat-for-entertainment explored in both Gladiator movies, in our world the idea of films pitting humans against humans has been around since 1932's The Most Dangerous Game &#x2014; and we've seen it all before, even if they haven't. True, the target audience for this wasn't alive for Logan's Run (1976), or The Running Man (1987), and would have certainly missed Bong Joon Ho's little-seen Snowpiercer (2013). But most would have been around for Edgar Wright's recent Running Man remake, which this film most closely resembles. Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

It begins in District 12 in almost Hillbilly Elegy levels of poverty-porn, with the first of several irrelevant chapter headings: "The Birthday." As we've already gathered, it is the 16 th birthday of Haymitch Abernathy ( Joseph Zada ), and, once again, the reaping is the dark shadow that falls across his special day. When one of the "tributes" tries to do a runner (and is killed in the process), Haymitch is forced to take his place, along with local girls Maysilee Donner ( McKenna Grace , who also plays her twin sister Merrilee) and Louella McCoy (Molly McCann), plus unpopular oddsmaker Wyatt Callow ( Ben Wang ). blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

They are all duly whisked off to the Capitol, where Haymitch is given a makeover by the lascivious fashionista Effie Trinket ( Elle Fanning ) and built up as the show's bad boy by overacting show host Caesar Flickerman ( Kieran Culkin ) &#x2014; who, admittedly, does have some good one-liners ("Look alive, children, while you still can!"). But the tributes' first outing during the opening parade goes horribly wrong when Louella is killed in a chariot crash, not that you'd notice much difference. She is quickly replaced by a lookalike, the equally inconsequential Lou Lou, but not before Haymitch upsets the vindictive, all-seeing, all-powerful President Coriolanus Snow (Ralph Fiennes), who sadistically does&#x2026; Well, nothing at all, or at least not just yet. Just you wait, Haymitch Abernathy!!!!

As he trains for the games and prepares to enter The Arena, Haymitch gets involved in a fiendish plot to sabotage the whole enterprise, something that involves a young newbie called Ampert and his father Beetee, who is being cruelly forced to mentor his son before watching his untimely death. "The Arena is a killing machine, but all machines can be broken," says Beetee, demonstrating this epochal moment by using a potato to turn a little fairy light on. In the meantime, Haymitch has found an unlikely potential ally in cameraman Plutarch Heavensbee ( Jesse Plemons ), who drops heavy hints that he is playing both ends against the middle and offers to turn a blind eye to the plan.

Playing Haymitch as nothing at all like the adult Woody Harrelson he will later grow up into, Zada has a na&#xEF;ve, doe-eyed charm that helps suspend disbelief for at least two hours of the film's increasingly tedious running time. That says a lot considering The Arena itself is quite ridiculously unscary, with its prospect of poisonous plants, electric butterflies and man-eating beavers &#x2014; or maybe rats, it was hard to tell even in Imax &#x2014; about as terrifying as being mauled to death by The Wonderful World of Disney . Worse, though, there is no sense of peril at all this time, even when Snow finally gets round to getting his drawn-out revenge: The Hunger Games has reached its High School Musical moment where everyone gets a participation prize, and any significant death is obviously just an unfortunate accident. The cast of the original film appear briefly at the end; one can't shake the feeling that it's to pay their last respects.

Title: The Hunger Games: Sunrise on the Reaping Distributor: Lionsgate Release date: November 20, 2026 Director: Francis Lawrence Screenwriters: Billy Ray, Michael Lesslie Cast: Joseph Zada, McKenna Grace, Ben Wang, Jesse Plemons, Elle Fanning, Kieran Culkin, Glenn Close , Billy Porter, Whitney Peak Rating: PG-13 Running time: 2 hrs 38 mins

EXCLUSIVE: Comedian Nate Jackson has announced dates for his 2027 Miracle Baby tour with Live Nation , kicking off February 5th in Cincinnati.

Tickets will be available starting with an artist presale beginning on Wednesday, November 18 at 10 a.m. local. Additional presales will run throughout the week ahead of the general on-sale beginning on Friday, November 20 at 10 a.m. local. View the full list of new dates below.

